    34	- **Why it works:** The familiar system is compressed into one glanceable object so its replacement can take the rest of the film; nothing here animates because nothing here is the subject.
    35	- **Evidence:** ev/t25-popular-vote-bar.jpg (ballot icon + split bar, 25 s)
    36	
    37	### T03 — Dot-grid identity system + withheld title
    38	- **Timestamps:** 0:28–0:37 (system recurs throughout; outro at T22)
    39	- **Mechanics:** A field of black dots on a newsprint-textured ground assembles; the words "Electoral College" land across it as red/blue split lettering. The same dot field is the substrate for the title, the threshold map (T07), and the outro. The title is withheld until 0:28 — after the misconception (T01) and the normal-system diagram (T02) are installed.
    40	- **Why it works:** One visual substance is the system itself: every later count is made of the same dots, so the viewer never has to learn a new representation. Withholding the title until the problem exists makes the title an answer, not a label.
    41	- **Evidence:** ev/t32-title-dotgrid.jpg (title over the dot field, 32 s) · Clip: clips/t03-grid-build.mp4 (28–36 s — the assembly order is the claim; stills cannot show it)
    42	
    43	### T04 — Vote-to-grid conversion diagram
    44	- **Timestamps:** 0:37–1:00
    45	- **Mechanics:** The ballot icon connects by a red bar to a grid of squares that fills red in blocks — a vote entering at left exits as allocated blocks at right. Operation: dot/square fill as conversion, drawn left-to-right in reading order.
    46	- **Why it works:** The film's central abstraction (your vote is converted, not counted) is drawn as a literal machine before any state is named.
    47	- **Evidence:** ev/t52-vote-to-grid.jpg (ballot icon → filling grid, 52 s)
    48	
    49	### T05 — Support-trend chart (Gallup line)
    50	- **Timestamps:** 0:54–1:07
    51	- **Mechanics:** A sparse line/dot chart, "Support for replacing the Electoral College" (Gallup polls 1967–2020), dots descending across decades. One chart, few marks, source named on the chart.
    52	- **Why it works:** The "most Americans dislike this" claim (transcript 0:54) is carried by a sourced trend, not by the narrator's say-so — the packet's first instance of its evidence habit (see T14, T18).
    53	- **Evidence:** ev/t60-gallup-chart.jpg (support trend chart, 60 s)
    54	
    55	### T06 — Capitol + representation dots (House basis)
